<p>Unlike Barcelona and several other major Spanish cities, <strong>Valencia does not currently have an active Zona de Bajas Emisiones (ZBE)</strong> in force. A planned zone covering roughly 27.8 km² of the city — bounded broadly by Ronda Norte, Avenida de los Naranjos, Serrería and Bulevar Sur — has been repeatedly delayed by a lack of political agreement on the implementing ordinance, and as of the start of 2026 it had not yet come into effect. This is worth knowing precisely because it's easy to assume every major Spanish city now enforces one; Valencia, at the time of writing, is a real exception. If and when the zone is approved, expect it to follow the pattern used elsewhere in Spain — restrictions on vehicles without an environmental label, enforced by camera, with fines around €200 for non-compliant vehicles. Anyone planning a longer stay or a rental car in Valencia should check the current status directly with the Ajuntament de València before travelling, since this is a live political situation rather than a settled rule.</p>

